          h: "Growing, fermentation and the puro culture",
          body: "Nicaraguan quality rests on painstaking fermentation and ageing. Green leaf harvested in the fields is hung from the beams of nearby curing barns and coloured and dried over some 45–60 days.\n【Fermentation (the pilón)】After curing, the leaves are stacked into mounds called 'pilones', where added moisture and their own weight generate heat within. This natural fermentation advances oxidation, drives off harsh components and ammonia, and polishes flavour and aroma. The process — temperatures watched with care, the layers rebuilt again and again — is said sometimes to run to more than a year in all.\n【The puro tradition】A cigar whose wrapper, binder and filler are all Nicaraguan leaf is called a 'puro'. Because leaf of differing character from the four regions can be combined two, three or four at a time, a puro can still achieve complexity without equal — reckoned one of Nicaragua's great strengths."
